-- Angel Digital 11+ — Migration 178
-- English Wave 1 Reading Remediation — Passage-Specific Question-Type
-- Variety (Founder Completion and Readiness Programme, Reading
-- Integrity Reconciliation, 2026-09-01).
--
-- ============================================================
-- WHY THIS MIGRATION EXISTS
-- ============================================================
-- The Reading Integrity Report (this session) independently confirmed
-- the Founder's suspicion: all 6 Wave 1 passages (migration 044) share
-- the EXACT same 7-question-type sequence in the EXACT same order —
-- QT-RC-01 (retrieval) -> QT-RC-03 (vocab-explain) -> QT-RC-04
-- (synonym-battery) -> QT-RC-02 (tick-justify, excluded from Practice —
-- see below) -> QT-RC-02 (quote-explain) -> QT-RC-06 (sequencing) ->
-- QT-RC-08 (emotion-cause) — a fixed architecture, not passage-specific
-- content design. A learner doing two Wave 1 passages learns the shape
-- of question 1 through 7 regardless of which passage they read.
--
-- ADDITIVE, NOT REPLACEMENT (Founder's explicit instruction: "Preserve
-- good existing questions where they meet the current standard" and
-- "DO NOT REBUILD GOOD CONTENT UNNECESSARILY"). Every one of the existing
-- 42 rows in migration 044 (36 live + 6 excluded tick-justify) is
-- UNTOUCHED by this migration — no row is deactivated, edited, or
-- removed. The Reading Passage Educational Quality Assessment (this
-- session) independently confirmed each existing question's own content
-- is genuinely good (real inference, real vocabulary, well-grounded
-- evidence) — the defect is architectural (identical sequence across
-- passages), not per-question quality. This migration ADDS 2 new,
-- passage-specific companion questions to EACH of the 6 passages (12
-- new rows total), each testing a reasoning demand ENTIRELY ABSENT from
-- Wave 1's own original architecture, chosen from what each passage's
-- own text specifically and naturally supports — not forced onto every
-- passage identically (the same defect this migration exists to fix).
--
-- Two demand types, neither present anywhere in the original Wave 1
-- architecture, are introduced: QT-RC-10 (Effect-of-Language/Word-Choice
-- Interpretation, competency RC-02) and QT-RC-07 (Multi-Entity
-- Comparative Attribute Extraction, competency RC-01) — both already
-- evidenced in docs/intelligence/CSSE_QUESTION_INTELLIGENCE_
-- FRAMEWORK.md, already used successfully elsewhere in this project
-- (RC-10: Wave 3, migration 063; RC-07: Wave 2, migration 049) but never
-- in Wave 1. A third, new family (motive/character-inference, QT-RC-02
-- competency but a genuinely distinct demand from both existing RC-02
-- sub-formats — asking WHY a character acts as they do, not asking for
-- a Yes/No judgement or a quotation-pair) is added where a passage's own
-- text supports it better than a comparative or effect-of-language
-- question would.
--
-- No two passages receive the identical PAIR of new types (see the
-- per-passage rationale below) — combined with each passage's own
-- distinct existing 6-question set, no two of the 6 passages now share
-- an identical overall question-type profile.
--
-- ============================================================
-- ANSWER-VALIDATION MECHANISM — DELIBERATELY NOT THE TICK-JUSTIFY
-- PATTERN
-- ============================================================
-- Every new question below uses `TIER2_ACCEPTED_SET` (an
-- `acceptedAnswers` list), the SAME deterministic-ish, reviewed, already-
-- live mechanism this project's own vocab-explain/direct-retrieval
-- families already use successfully — never the `TIER3_QUOTATION_PLUS_
-- EXPLANATION` self-assessed "tick Yes/No, then give two reasons" shape
-- that `wave1-fam-tick-justify` uses. This is a deliberate, disclosed
-- choice: the Reading Integrity Report found a real, documented
-- self-assessment-validity incident (`w1-raceday-05`, a sibling
-- TIER3-quotation-tier row, where a deliberately inadequate answer was
-- self-assessed as correct) — new content this migration adds does not
-- reuse or extend that risk pattern in any form. No tick-justify row is
-- touched, recovered, or referenced anywhere in this migration.
--
-- Every quotation used in any new question below was independently
-- verified this session to appear verbatim in its own passage's stored
-- text (matching migration 044's own established discipline) — checked
-- directly against the passage text embedded in migration 044 before
-- this file was written, not assumed.
--
-- ============================================================
-- PER-PASSAGE RATIONALE (why these two new types, for this passage
-- specifically — arising from the text, not forced)
-- ============================================================
-- The Kite Maker (relationship-skill): RC-10 on "flap a kite about"
-- (Grandad's own dismissive metaphor for careless flying) + a new
-- motive-inference question on WHY Grandad withholds help — the
-- passage's own central theme, previously only tested via a quotation-
-- pairing task (Q5), not a direct reasoning-about-motive task.
--
-- The Last Bus (tension-firstperson): RC-10 on "gulping air like
-- something that had been underwater too long" (a striking simile the
-- original 7 questions never touch) + RC-07 comparing the narrator's
-- physical/emotional state at the very start vs the very end of the
-- passage — a natural fit for this passage's own before/after physical
-- arc (running in fear -> laughing in relief).
--
-- The New Girl (internal-external-contrast): RC-07 comparing Priya's
-- REHEARSED opening line against what she ACTUALLY says ("Is the pasta
-- usually this cold?") — the passage's own explicit internal/external
-- contrast, previously untested directly + a new motive-inference
-- question on why the boy's casual response helps Priya, even though
-- "Nobody had asked where she was from."
--
-- The Attic Door (descriptive-suspense): RC-10 on the personification
-- "a groan so long and so mournful that Marcus half expected something
-- to groan back" + a new motive-inference question on why Marcus pauses
-- before lifting the lid ("savouring... the last few seconds of not yet
-- knowing") — both draw on this passage's own distinctively rich
-- atmospheric language, its strongest asset per the independent quality
-- assessment.
--
-- Race Day (two-character-contrast): RC-07 comparing Ade's and Cass's
-- preparation styles directly (the passage's own explicit organising
-- contrast) + RC-10 on the simile "she ran the way some people did
-- crosswords: unbothered, half-distracted" — both arise from this
-- passage's own central two-character structure.
--
-- A Letter to Nana (epistolary-sequence): RC-07 comparing how Dara
-- describes Bristol/homesickness at the start of the letter against how
-- she describes it by the end ("I still miss home, but slightly less
-- loudly than I did in September") — implicit change-over-time the
-- original 7 questions never test directly + a new motive-inference
-- question on why Dara appreciated the launderette woman's help "more
-- than she probably realised."
--
-- ============================================================
-- WHAT THIS MIGRATION DOES NOT DO
-- ============================================================
-- Does not touch, edit, deactivate, or reference any of migration 044's
-- 42 existing rows. Does not recover, reference, or modify any
-- `wave1-fam-tick-justify` row. Does not touch Wave 2 or Wave 3 content
-- (migrations 049/051/063). Does not set eligibility_status to anything
-- other than 'provisional' (matching migration 044's own original
-- convention for this wave, since this is genuinely new, not-yet-
-- reviewed content in the same wave). Does not create or touch any
-- ali_family_review row (a separate pending-review migration follows,
-- consolidated with the Wave 3 remediation). Does not activate anything.
--
-- FAIL-CLOSED / DUPLICATE-ID PROTECTION: `on conflict (id) do nothing`.
--
-- NOT APPLIED. Generated for Founder review and manual application via
-- Supabase Dashboard > SQL Editor > New query.

