-- THE KITE MAKER
('w1-kitemaker-08', 'english', 'QT-RC-10', array['csse'], 'medium', 'short-answer', 90,
 $json${"id":"w1-kitemaker-08","marks":2,"skill":"language","question":"Grandad says, \"Anyone can flap a kite about and call it flying.\" What does the word 'flap' suggest about how Grandad views careless kite-flying?","modelAnswer":"'Flap' suggests clumsy, uncontrolled, undignified movement -- the opposite of the careful, precise flying Grandad values and teaches Femi.","passageTitle":"The Kite Maker","passageText":"Grandad Owusu never rushed a kite. He said a kite built in a hurry would fly like it was built in a hurry: badly, and not for long. Every Saturday morning, while the rest of the house was still deciding whether to get up, he and Femi sat at the kitchen table with a pile of bamboo strips, a ball of string, and a stack of old newspapers that Grandad kept folded in a box marked, in faded pen, KITES ONLY.\n\nFemi had watched him make a dozen kites before he was allowed to touch the bamboo himself. Grandad said this was not because Femi was too young, but because watching properly was its own kind of work. \"Anyone can flap a kite about and call it flying,\" he told Femi, snipping a length of string with unhurried precision. \"Only a few people notice why one kite climbs and another one drops into the hedge.\"\n\nThis Saturday, Grandad finally slid a bundle of bamboo strips across the table. \"Your turn,\" he said. Femi's hands, usually so quick at video games, felt suddenly clumsy around the thin wood. He crossed two strips the way he had seen Grandad do it a hundred times, but the shape came out lopsided, one arm longer than the other.\n\nHe waited for Grandad to fix it. Grandad did not move.\n\n\"It's wrong,\" Femi said.\n\n\"It's a start,\" Grandad said. \"Different thing entirely.\" He tapped the longer arm with one finger, not touching it, just pointing. \"What do you notice?\"\n\nFemi looked. He measured the two arms with his eyes, then with his fingers. \"This one's longer.\"\n\n\"So?\"\n\n\"So it won't balance.\"\n\n\"So?\"\n\nFemi sighed, but he was smiling now, because this was the game Grandad always played, the one where he refused to simply say the answer. Femi trimmed the longer arm himself, checked it again, and this time the cross sat even. He looked up, half expecting praise, but Grandad only nodded once, as if Femi had merely confirmed something Grandad already knew.\n\nIt took Femi three more attempts before the frame held its shape without sagging. His fingers ached from pulling the string tight, and twice he had to unpick a knot that had gone stubborn and tangled. When the frame was finally finished, Grandad held it up to the window and turned it slowly, checking the balance against the light.\n\n\"Well,\" he said at last. \"That'll fly.\"\n\nFemi realised, watching Grandad's face, that this was the closest thing to a celebration he was going to get, and that somehow it was enough.","acceptedAnswers":["clumsy or careless movement","uncontrolled, not proper flying","suggests carelessness or lack of skill","messy, undignified movement"],"validationTier":"TIER2_ACCEPTED_SET"}$json$,
 'English Wave 1 Reading Remediation (Founder Reading Integrity Reconciliation, 2026-09-01). QT-RC-10 (Effect-of-Language/Word-Choice Interpretation), competency RC-02. Question family: wave1-fam-effect-of-language. New demand type for Wave 1 -- see migration header for the per-passage rationale. Quote independently re-verified verbatim against the stored passage text.', 2, 'wave1-eng-kitemaker',
 'wave1-fam-effect-of-language', 'angel_original', 'provisional', 1, true, 'Treating ''flap'' as a neutral synonym for ''fly'' rather than recognising its deliberately clumsy, dismissive connotation.',
 'NEAR_TRANSFER', null),

('w1-kitemaker-09', 'english', 'QT-RC-02', array['csse'], 'medium', 'short-answer', 90,
 $json${"id":"w1-kitemaker-09","marks":2,"skill":"inference","question":"Why does Grandad wait for Femi to notice and fix the lopsided kite frame himself, instead of fixing it or explaining the problem straight away?","modelAnswer":"Grandad believes that working things out for yourself is genuine learning -- he wants Femi to notice problems and reason through them independently, not simply be told or shown the answer.","passageTitle":"The Kite Maker","passageText":"Grandad Owusu never rushed a kite. He said a kite built in a hurry would fly like it was built in a hurry: badly, and not for long. Every Saturday morning, while the rest of the house was still deciding whether to get up, he and Femi sat at the kitchen table with a pile of bamboo strips, a ball of string, and a stack of old newspapers that Grandad kept folded in a box marked, in faded pen, KITES ONLY.\n\nFemi had watched him make a dozen kites before he was allowed to touch the bamboo himself. Grandad said this was not because Femi was too young, but because watching properly was its own kind of work. \"Anyone can flap a kite about and call it flying,\" he told Femi, snipping a length of string with unhurried precision. \"Only a few people notice why one kite climbs and another one drops into the hedge.\"\n\nThis Saturday, Grandad finally slid a bundle of bamboo strips across the table. \"Your turn,\" he said. Femi's hands, usually so quick at video games, felt suddenly clumsy around the thin wood. He crossed two strips the way he had seen Grandad do it a hundred times, but the shape came out lopsided, one arm longer than the other.\n\nHe waited for Grandad to fix it. Grandad did not move.\n\n\"It's wrong,\" Femi said.\n\n\"It's a start,\" Grandad said. \"Different thing entirely.\" He tapped the longer arm with one finger, not touching it, just pointing. \"What do you notice?\"\n\nFemi looked. He measured the two arms with his eyes, then with his fingers. \"This one's longer.\"\n\n\"So?\"\n\n\"So it won't balance.\"\n\n\"So?\"\n\nFemi sighed, but he was smiling now, because this was the game Grandad always played, the one where he refused to simply say the answer. Femi trimmed the longer arm himself, checked it again, and this time the cross sat even. He looked up, half expecting praise, but Grandad only nodded once, as if Femi had merely confirmed something Grandad already knew.\n\nIt took Femi three more attempts before the frame held its shape without sagging. His fingers ached from pulling the string tight, and twice he had to unpick a knot that had gone stubborn and tangled. When the frame was finally finished, Grandad held it up to the window and turned it slowly, checking the balance against the light.\n\n\"Well,\" he said at last. \"That'll fly.\"\n\nFemi realised, watching Grandad's face, that this was the closest thing to a celebration he was going to get, and that somehow it was enough.","acceptedAnswers":["so Femi learns to notice and solve problems himself","wants Femi to reason it out rather than be told","believes working it out yourself is real learning","testing/encouraging Femi's own understanding"],"validationTier":"TIER2_ACCEPTED_SET"}$json$,
 'English Wave 1 Reading Remediation. QT-RC-02 (motive/character inference sub-format -- distinct from both existing wave1-fam-tick-justify and wave1-fam-quote-explain), competency RC-02. Question family: wave1-fam-motive-inference. Deliberately uses TIER2_ACCEPTED_SET, not the tick-justify self-assessment mechanism -- see migration header.', 2, 'wave1-eng-kitemaker',
 'wave1-fam-motive-inference', 'angel_original', 'provisional', 1, true, 'Describing WHAT Grandad does (waits, stays still) without explaining WHY -- the passage requires reasoning about his underlying teaching philosophy, not just re-describing his actions.',
 'FAR_TRANSFER', null),

-- THE LAST BUS
('w1-lastbus-08', 'english', 'QT-RC-10', array['csse'], 'medium', 'short-answer', 90,
 $json${"id":"w1-lastbus-08","marks":2,"skill":"language","question":"The narrator describes herself \"gulping air like something that had been underwater too long.\" What does this comparison suggest about how she feels at that moment?","modelAnswer":"It suggests she is desperately out of breath and physically exhausted from running -- gasping for air urgently, as if she had genuinely been deprived of it, emphasising just how hard and how far she had been forced to run.","passageTitle":"The Last Bus","passageText":"I had exactly six minutes to reach the bus stop on Ferry Road, and I was still four streets away when my shoelace came undone for the second time that morning.\n\nI didn't stop. I told myself the lace could wait, the way you tell yourself lots of things can wait when you are already running. My bag thumped against my back with every stride, and somewhere inside it my recorder case was rattling loose against my water bottle, a sound that seemed, absurdly, to be counting down the minutes along with me.\n\nAt the corner of Wren Street I nearly collided with a man walking a dog nearly as tall as I was. He said something I didn't catch. I said sorry without really meaning it, because sorry was easier than stopping to explain that if I missed the 8:14 there would not be another bus for forty minutes, and forty minutes was exactly long enough to turn me from a girl who was occasionally a little late into a girl who had missed her audition entirely.\n\nI could see the stop now, a low glass shelter with three people already waiting under it. I couldn't see the bus. That was something, at least. I slowed just slightly, enough to breathe, enough to feel my heart doing something urgent and uneven in my chest.\n\nThen I saw it: a flash of red at the far end of Ferry Road, still small with distance, but unmistakably a bus, unmistakably mine.\n\nI ran the last stretch properly then, lace flapping, bag thumping, arms pumping in a way that would have embarrassed me on any other morning. The three people at the stop turned to watch me coming, which embarrassed me anyway. I reached the shelter exactly as the bus doors hissed open, and for a moment I could not speak at all, could only stand there with my hands on my knees, gulping air like something that had been underwater too long.\n\nThe driver waited. He didn't have to, but he did, watching me in his mirror with an expression I couldn't read, somewhere between amusement and patience.\n\n\"Cutting it fine,\" he said, as I finally climbed the steps.\n\n\"Every time,\" I managed, and found, to my surprise, that I was laughing, actual proper laughing, the kind that comes when your body has been too frightened to laugh for the last six minutes and has finally been given permission.","acceptedAnswers":["desperately out of breath / gasping for air","physically exhausted from running so hard","urgent, extreme need to breathe","emphasises how hard she had been running"],"validationTier":"TIER2_ACCEPTED_SET"}$json$,
 'English Wave 1 Reading Remediation. QT-RC-10, competency RC-02. Question family: wave1-fam-effect-of-language. New demand type for Wave 1. Quote independently re-verified verbatim.', 2, 'wave1-eng-lastbus',
 'wave1-fam-effect-of-language', 'angel_original', 'provisional', 1, true, 'Interpreting the simile literally (thinking she was actually near water) rather than recognising it as a comparison for breathlessness.',
 'NEAR_TRANSFER', null),

('w1-lastbus-09', 'english', 'QT-RC-07', array['csse'], 'medium', 'short-answer', 100,
 $json${"id":"w1-lastbus-09","marks":2,"skill":"comparison","question":"Compare how the narrator feels at the very start of the passage with how she feels at the very end. How has her feeling changed?","modelAnswer":"At the start she is anxious and rushed, panicking about missing the bus. By the end, once she has safely caught it, her fear turns into relief and she finds herself genuinely laughing -- a complete shift from fear to relief and amusement.","passageTitle":"The Last Bus","passageText":"I had exactly six minutes to reach the bus stop on Ferry Road, and I was still four streets away when my shoelace came undone for the second time that morning.\n\nI didn't stop. I told myself the lace could wait, the way you tell yourself lots of things can wait when you are already running. My bag thumped against my back with every stride, and somewhere inside it my recorder case was rattling loose against my water bottle, a sound that seemed, absurdly, to be counting down the minutes along with me.\n\nAt the corner of Wren Street I nearly collided with a man walking a dog nearly as tall as I was. He said something I didn't catch. I said sorry without really meaning it, because sorry was easier than stopping to explain that if I missed the 8:14 there would not be another bus for forty minutes, and forty minutes was exactly long enough to turn me from a girl who was occasionally a little late into a girl who had missed her audition entirely.\n\nI could see the stop now, a low glass shelter with three people already waiting under it. I couldn't see the bus. That was something, at least. I slowed just slightly, enough to breathe, enough to feel my heart doing something urgent and uneven in my chest.\n\nThen I saw it: a flash of red at the far end of Ferry Road, still small with distance, but unmistakably a bus, unmistakably mine.\n\nI ran the last stretch properly then, lace flapping, bag thumping, arms pumping in a way that would have embarrassed me on any other morning. The three people at the stop turned to watch me coming, which embarrassed me anyway. I reached the shelter exactly as the bus doors hissed open, and for a moment I could not speak at all, could only stand there with my hands on my knees, gulping air like something that had been underwater too long.\n\nThe driver waited. He didn't have to, but he did, watching me in his mirror with an expression I couldn't read, somewhere between amusement and patience.\n\n\"Cutting it fine,\" he said, as I finally climbed the steps.\n\n\"Every time,\" I managed, and found, to my surprise, that I was laughing, actual proper laughing, the kind that comes when your body has been too frightened to laugh for the last six minutes and has finally been given permission.","acceptedAnswers":["from anxious/panicked to relieved and laughing","fear turns to relief and amusement","goes from frightened to happy/laughing once safe","panic at the start, relief and laughter by the end"],"validationTier":"TIER2_ACCEPTED_SET"}$json$,
 'English Wave 1 Reading Remediation. QT-RC-07 (Multi-Entity Comparative Attribute Extraction, applied here to the same character at two different points rather than two different characters), competency RC-01. Question family: wave1-fam-comparative-extraction. New demand type for Wave 1.', 2, 'wave1-eng-lastbus',
 'wave1-fam-comparative-extraction', 'angel_original', 'provisional', 1, true, 'Describing only one end of the emotional arc (e.g. only the fear, or only the relief) rather than the CHANGE between the two states, which is what the question asks for.',
 'FAR_TRANSFER', null),

-- THE NEW GIRL
('w1-newgirl-08', 'english', 'QT-RC-07', array['csse'], 'medium', 'short-answer', 100,
 $json${"id":"w1-newgirl-08","marks":2,"skill":"comparison","question":"Compare the sentence Priya had rehearsed to say with what she actually says when the boy invites her to sit with him. What is the difference?","modelAnswer":"Priya had rehearsed a breezy sentence about moving from Leicester and the school smelling of disinfectant. What she actually says is completely unplanned and ordinary: \"Is the pasta usually this cold?\" -- the rehearsed line never gets used at all.","passageTitle":"The New Girl","passageText":"Priya had rehearsed exactly what she would say when someone finally spoke to her. She had practised it twice in the mirror that morning, a short, breezy sentence about how she'd just moved from Leicester and wasn't it funny how every school smelled slightly of the same disinfectant. It was, she had decided, a good opening line: friendly, a little funny, not desperate.\n\nBy lunchtime she had not used it once.\n\nShe sat at the end of a long table in the dining hall, her tray positioned with unnecessary precision in front of her, and watched the room organise itself into groups she did not belong to. Nobody was unkind. That was almost the strangest part. A girl with a long plait had smiled at her in the corridor that morning, a genuine smile, and said \"you're the new one, right?\" before being swept away by a wave of friends before Priya could answer. It should have felt like an opening. Instead it felt like a door that had opened and closed again before she could step through it.\n\n\"You can sit with us, if you want.\"\n\nPriya looked up. It was a boy from her form group, though she couldn't remember his name, gesturing vaguely at a half-empty table nearby. His tone was so casual that for a moment she wondered if he'd meant to say it to someone else entirely.\n\n\"Thanks,\" she said, and heard, with faint horror, that her voice had come out smaller than she'd intended.\n\nShe picked up her tray. Her carefully rehearsed sentence about Leicester and disinfectant surfaced in her mind, fully formed, ready to be used. She opened her mouth. What came out instead was, \"Is the pasta usually this cold?\"\n\nThe boy laughed, an easy, unbothered laugh, and said the pasta was always this cold, it was basically a school tradition by now, and somehow that was enough. Nobody had asked where she was from. Nobody needed to, not yet. She sat down at the edge of the group, said very little for the rest of lunch, and found, to her own surprise, that she didn't mind. It was, she thought, a start.","acceptedAnswers":["rehearsed line never used; asks about the pasta instead","planned a witty introduction but said something ordinary/unplanned","actual words are completely different from the rehearsed sentence","she meant to say something clever but blurted out a random question"],"validationTier":"TIER2_ACCEPTED_SET"}$json$,
 'English Wave 1 Reading Remediation. QT-RC-07, competency RC-01. Question family: wave1-fam-comparative-extraction. New demand type for Wave 1, drawing directly on this passage''s own internal/external contrast theme.', 2, 'wave1-eng-newgirl',
 'wave1-fam-comparative-extraction', 'angel_original', 'provisional', 1, true, 'Only describing the rehearsed line OR only describing what she actually said, without explicitly contrasting the two, which is what the question requires.',
 'FAR_TRANSFER', null),

('w1-newgirl-09', 'english', 'QT-RC-02', array['csse'], 'medium', 'short-answer', 90,
 $json${"id":"w1-newgirl-09","marks":2,"skill":"inference","question":"Why does the passage say \"Nobody had asked where she was from. Nobody needed to, not yet\" felt like enough for Priya?","modelAnswer":"Priya had been anxious about explaining herself and being the centre of attention as \"the new girl\". Being included casually, without needing to explain her background at all, let her feel accepted for something ordinary (sitting together, talking about the food) rather than singled out as different.","passageTitle":"The New Girl","passageText":"Priya had rehearsed exactly what she would say when someone finally spoke to her. She had practised it twice in the mirror that morning, a short, breezy sentence about how she'd just moved from Leicester and wasn't it funny how every school smelled slightly of the same disinfectant. It was, she had decided, a good opening line: friendly, a little funny, not desperate.\n\nBy lunchtime she had not used it once.\n\nShe sat at the end of a long table in the dining hall, her tray positioned with unnecessary precision in front of her, and watched the room organise itself into groups she did not belong to. Nobody was unkind. That was almost the strangest part. A girl with a long plait had smiled at her in the corridor that morning, a genuine smile, and said \"you're the new one, right?\" before being swept away by a wave of friends before Priya could answer. It should have felt like an opening. Instead it felt like a door that had opened and closed again before she could step through it.\n\n\"You can sit with us, if you want.\"\n\nPriya looked up. It was a boy from her form group, though she couldn't remember his name, gesturing vaguely at a half-empty table nearby. His tone was so casual that for a moment she wondered if he'd meant to say it to someone else entirely.\n\n\"Thanks,\" she said, and heard, with faint horror, that her voice had come out smaller than she'd intended.\n\nShe picked up her tray. Her carefully rehearsed sentence about Leicester and disinfectant surfaced in her mind, fully formed, ready to be used. She opened her mouth. What came out instead was, \"Is the pasta usually this cold?\"\n\nThe boy laughed, an easy, unbothered laugh, and said the pasta was always this cold, it was basically a school tradition by now, and somehow that was enough. Nobody had asked where she was from. Nobody needed to, not yet. She sat down at the edge of the group, said very little for the rest of lunch, and found, to her own surprise, that she didn't mind. It was, she thought, a start.","acceptedAnswers":["felt accepted as ordinary rather than singled out as the new/different girl","did not have to explain or perform being new","being included casually mattered more than being noticed","let her belong without having to justify herself"],"validationTier":"TIER2_ACCEPTED_SET"}$json$,
 'English Wave 1 Reading Remediation. QT-RC-02 (motive/character inference sub-format), competency RC-02. Question family: wave1-fam-motive-inference.', 2, 'wave1-eng-newgirl',
 'wave1-fam-motive-inference', 'angel_original', 'provisional', 1, true, 'Assuming Priya simply forgot her rehearsed line by accident, missing that the passage frames the casual, low-pressure inclusion itself as what mattered to her.',
 'FAR_TRANSFER', null),

-- THE ATTIC DOOR
('w1-atticdoor-08', 'english', 'QT-RC-10', array['csse'], 'hard', 'short-answer', 100,
 $json${"id":"w1-atticdoor-08","marks":2,"skill":"language","question":"The door swings open \"with a groan so long and so mournful that Marcus half expected something to groan back.\" What effect does describing the door as if it could \"groan back\" create?","modelAnswer":"It makes the door (and the attic) feel almost alive or eerie, as though something might genuinely respond -- creating a tense, slightly unsettling atmosphere of anticipation before Marcus enters.","passageTitle":"The Attic Door","passageText":"The attic door had been painted shut for as long as Marcus could remember, its hinges buried under so many layers of old white gloss that it had stopped looking like a door at all and started looking like part of the wall. Nobody in the house had opened it in years. Nobody, until this particular wet Tuesday, had especially wanted to.\n\nMarcus wanted to. He had wanted to for exactly eleven days, ever since his grandmother had mentioned, almost carelessly, that his great-grandfather's old travelling trunk was still up there somewhere, gathering dust and, in her words, \"several decades' worth of nonsense nobody's had the courage to sort through.\"\n\nHe worked a flat-bladed knife into the seam where the door met the frame, feeling the old paint crack and splinter under the pressure. A fine white dust drifted down and settled on his sleeve. When the door finally gave way, it did so all at once, swinging inward with a groan so long and so mournful that Marcus half expected something to groan back.\n\nNothing did. The attic beyond was dim, lit only by a single grimy window at the far end, and thick with the particular smell of old houses: dust and damp wood and something sweetish underneath, like forgotten paper slowly turning to nothing. Shapes crouched under grey dust sheets, furniture from decades Marcus couldn't guess at, their outlines softened and strange.\n\nHe picked his way across the boards, testing each one before he trusted it with his full weight, until he reached a low shape in the far corner that seemed, unmistakably, trunk-shaped. He pulled the sheet away in one motion, sending a fresh cloud of dust spiralling into the grey light.\n\nThe trunk was smaller than he'd imagined, its leather straps cracked and pale, its brass corners dulled almost black with age. A tarnished padlock hung from the clasp, but when Marcus touched it, it simply fell open in his hand, as though it, too, had been waiting eleven days, or perhaps eleven years, for someone to finally come and ask.\n\nHe knelt in front of it for a long moment before he lifted the lid, savouring, in a way he couldn't quite explain, the last few seconds of not yet knowing.","acceptedAnswers":["makes the door/attic feel alive or eerie","creates a tense, unsettling atmosphere","suggests something might be about to happen/respond","builds suspense before Marcus enters"],"validationTier":"TIER2_ACCEPTED_SET"}$json$,
 'English Wave 1 Reading Remediation. QT-RC-10, competency RC-02. Question family: wave1-fam-effect-of-language. New demand type for Wave 1, drawing on this passage''s own strongest asset (atmospheric personification), per the independent quality assessment.', 2, 'wave1-eng-atticdoor',
 'wave1-fam-effect-of-language', 'angel_original', 'provisional', 1, true, 'Describing the sound only literally (e.g. "the door was loud") without engaging with the personification -- what the comparison to a living groan suggests about atmosphere.',
 'NEAR_TRANSFER', null),

('w1-atticdoor-09', 'english', 'QT-RC-02', array['csse'], 'hard', 'short-answer', 90,
 $json${"id":"w1-atticdoor-09","marks":2,"skill":"inference","question":"Why does Marcus pause \"for a long moment\" before lifting the lid of the trunk, \"savouring... the last few seconds of not yet knowing\"?","modelAnswer":"He has waited eleven days to reach this moment, and once the lid is open the mystery will be solved -- he wants to prolong the excitement and anticipation of not yet knowing what is inside, rather than rushing to find out.","passageTitle":"The Attic Door","passageText":"The attic door had been painted shut for as long as Marcus could remember, its hinges buried under so many layers of old white gloss that it had stopped looking like a door at all and started looking like part of the wall. Nobody in the house had opened it in years. Nobody, until this particular wet Tuesday, had especially wanted to.\n\nMarcus wanted to. He had wanted to for exactly eleven days, ever since his grandmother had mentioned, almost carelessly, that his great-grandfather's old travelling trunk was still up there somewhere, gathering dust and, in her words, \"several decades' worth of nonsense nobody's had the courage to sort through.\"\n\nHe worked a flat-bladed knife into the seam where the door met the frame, feeling the old paint crack and splinter under the pressure. A fine white dust drifted down and settled on his sleeve. When the door finally gave way, it did so all at once, swinging inward with a groan so long and so mournful that Marcus half expected something to groan back.\n\nNothing did. The attic beyond was dim, lit only by a single grimy window at the far end, and thick with the particular smell of old houses: dust and damp wood and something sweetish underneath, like forgotten paper slowly turning to nothing. Shapes crouched under grey dust sheets, furniture from decades Marcus couldn't guess at, their outlines softened and strange.\n\nHe picked his way across the boards, testing each one before he trusted it with his full weight, until he reached a low shape in the far corner that seemed, unmistakably, trunk-shaped. He pulled the sheet away in one motion, sending a fresh cloud of dust spiralling into the grey light.\n\nThe trunk was smaller than he'd imagined, its leather straps cracked and pale, its brass corners dulled almost black with age. A tarnished padlock hung from the clasp, but when Marcus touched it, it simply fell open in his hand, as though it, too, had been waiting eleven days, or perhaps eleven years, for someone to finally come and ask.\n\nHe knelt in front of it for a long moment before he lifted the lid, savouring, in a way he couldn't quite explain, the last few seconds of not yet knowing.","acceptedAnswers":["wants to prolong the excitement/anticipation before finding out","enjoys the suspense of not knowing yet","the mystery will end once he looks, so he delays it briefly","savouring the moment of anticipation after waiting so long"],"validationTier":"TIER2_ACCEPTED_SET"}$json$,
 'English Wave 1 Reading Remediation. QT-RC-02 (motive/character inference sub-format), competency RC-02. Question family: wave1-fam-motive-inference.', 2, 'wave1-eng-atticdoor',
 'wave1-fam-motive-inference', 'angel_original', 'provisional', 1, true, 'Assuming Marcus is nervous or afraid, when the passage explicitly frames the pause as pleasurable anticipation ("savouring"), not fear.',
 'FAR_TRANSFER', null),

-- RACE DAY
('w1-raceday-08', 'english', 'QT-RC-07', array['csse'], 'medium', 'short-answer', 100,
 $json${"id":"w1-raceday-08","marks":2,"skill":"comparison","question":"Compare how Ade and Cass each prepare for the relay race. What is the main difference between them?","modelAnswer":"Ade prepares intensely and methodically -- arriving two hours early, doing deliberate warm-up laps, checking his spikes repeatedly, and reading his split times twice. Cass prepares casually -- arriving only forty minutes early, eating half a banana, and lying down with her cap over her eyes instead of visibly warming up.","passageTitle":"Race Day","passageText":"Two hours before the county relay, Ade was already at the track, jogging slow, deliberate laps to loosen muscles that did not need loosening, checking his spikes for the fourth time, running through the handover with an imaginary baton held out at exactly the right angle. He had a laminated card in his kit bag listing his split times for the last six meets, and he had read it twice already that morning, as if the numbers might have changed overnight.\n\nCass arrived forty minutes before the race, ate half a banana, and lay down on the grass with her cap over her eyes.\n\n\"You're not warming up,\" Ade said, not quite managing to keep the disapproval out of his voice.\n\n\"I warmed up on the walk here,\" Cass said, without moving the cap.\n\nAde found this deeply unconvincing, though he had learned, over two seasons of running the same relay leg as Cass, that arguing about it achieved nothing. She ran the way some people did crosswords: unbothered, half-distracted, and somehow, infuriatingly, faster than people who took it seriously.\n\nWhen their event was finally called, Ade felt the familiar tightening in his chest, the one that had nothing to do with his lungs and everything to do with the six laminated split times folded in his pocket. Cass, jogging beside him to the start line, was humming something under her breath that he didn't recognise.\n\nThe gun went. Ade ran his leg exactly as planned, matching his practised splits almost to the second, and handed off cleanly to the third runner with the baton secure and his form textbook-perfect. He allowed himself, for the first time all morning, a small breath of relief.\n\nCass ran the anchor leg the way she seemed to do everything: as though the outcome had already been decided somewhere she wasn't especially interested in checking. She crossed the line first by four strides, not visibly out of breath, and spent the next several minutes being hugged by teammates who were considerably more excited about it than she appeared to be.\n\nAde watched her from a short distance, his own relief from moments earlier curdling slightly into something less comfortable. He had done everything right. She had simply won.","acceptedAnswers":["Ade prepares intensely/methodically, Cass prepares casually","Ade is anxious and thorough, Cass is relaxed and minimal","Ade arrives early and checks everything, Cass arrives late and relaxes","one is highly organised, the other seems unbothered"],"validationTier":"TIER2_ACCEPTED_SET"}$json$,
 'English Wave 1 Reading Remediation. QT-RC-07, competency RC-01. Question family: wave1-fam-comparative-extraction. Drawing directly on this passage''s own central two-character contrast.', 2, 'wave1-eng-raceday',
 'wave1-fam-comparative-extraction', 'angel_original', 'provisional', 1, true, 'Describing only one character''s approach without explicitly contrasting it against the other, which is what the question requires.',
 'FAR_TRANSFER', null),

('w1-raceday-09', 'english', 'QT-RC-10', array['csse'], 'medium', 'short-answer', 90,
 $json${"id":"w1-raceday-09","marks":2,"skill":"language","question":"The passage says Cass \"ran the way some people did crosswords: unbothered, half-distracted.\" What does comparing her running to doing a crossword suggest about her attitude?","modelAnswer":"It suggests Cass treats running as a casual, almost relaxing activity rather than a serious challenge -- something she does without visible effort or stress, unlike a competitive event she is nervous about.","passageTitle":"Race Day","passageText":"Two hours before the county relay, Ade was already at the track, jogging slow, deliberate laps to loosen muscles that did not need loosening, checking his spikes for the fourth time, running through the handover with an imaginary baton held out at exactly the right angle. He had a laminated card in his kit bag listing his split times for the last six meets, and he had read it twice already that morning, as if the numbers might have changed overnight.\n\nCass arrived forty minutes before the race, ate half a banana, and lay down on the grass with her cap over her eyes.\n\n\"You're not warming up,\" Ade said, not quite managing to keep the disapproval out of his voice.\n\n\"I warmed up on the walk here,\" Cass said, without moving the cap.\n\nAde found this deeply unconvincing, though he had learned, over two seasons of running the same relay leg as Cass, that arguing about it achieved nothing. She ran the way some people did crosswords: unbothered, half-distracted, and somehow, infuriatingly, faster than people who took it seriously.\n\nWhen their event was finally called, Ade felt the familiar tightening in his chest, the one that had nothing to do with his lungs and everything to do with the six laminated split times folded in his pocket. Cass, jogging beside him to the start line, was humming something under her breath that he didn't recognise.\n\nThe gun went. Ade ran his leg exactly as planned, matching his practised splits almost to the second, and handed off cleanly to the third runner with the baton secure and his form textbook-perfect. He allowed himself, for the first time all morning, a small breath of relief.\n\nCass ran the anchor leg the way she seemed to do everything: as though the outcome had already been decided somewhere she wasn't especially interested in checking. She crossed the line first by four strides, not visibly out of breath, and spent the next several minutes being hugged by teammates who were considerably more excited about it than she appeared to be.\n\nAde watched her from a short distance, his own relief from moments earlier curdling slightly into something less comfortable. He had done everything right. She had simply won.","acceptedAnswers":["treats running as casual/relaxing, not stressful","unbothered, low-effort attitude","doesn''t take it seriously the way Ade does","calm, almost effortless approach"],"validationTier":"TIER2_ACCEPTED_SET"}$json$,
 'English Wave 1 Reading Remediation. QT-RC-10, competency RC-02. Question family: wave1-fam-effect-of-language.', 2, 'wave1-eng-raceday',
 'wave1-fam-effect-of-language', 'angel_original', 'provisional', 1, true, 'Focusing on crosswords being difficult/clever rather than on the CASUAL, unbothered way the comparison suggests Cass approaches running.',
