-- A LETTER TO NANA
('w1-letter-08', 'english', 'QT-RC-07', array['csse'], 'medium', 'short-answer', 100,
 $json${"id":"w1-letter-08","marks":2,"skill":"comparison","question":"Compare how Dara describes missing home at the start of the letter with how she describes it in the final paragraph. What has changed?","modelAnswer":"Early in the letter Dara focuses on how strange, loud and unfamiliar Bristol feels, and getting lost, embarrassed, on her first day. By the final paragraph she admits she still misses home, but says it is \"slightly less loudly than I did in September\" -- showing her homesickness has eased a little over time, even though it hasn't disappeared completely.","passageTitle":"A Letter to Nana","passageText":"Dear Nana,\n\nMum says I have to write to you properly instead of just sending you funny videos, so here is a proper letter, even though I still think the videos were better.\n\nBristol is loud in a different way from home. Not louder exactly, just loud about different things. There is a market near our new flat that starts setting up at six in the morning, and for the first week I woke up every single day thinking someone was dismantling a small building directly under my window. Mum says I will stop noticing it eventually. I do not fully believe her yet, but she was right about the flat being bigger than it looked in the photos, so maybe she will be right about this too.\n\nThe first proper thing that happened was that I got lost on the way to school, on my actual first day, which is possibly the most embarrassing thing that has ever happened to me, and I am including the time I fell off the stage in Year 4. I took a left turn that I was extremely confident about and ended up outside a launderette instead of the school gates. A woman doing her washing pointed me the right way without laughing at me even once, which I appreciated more than she probably realised.\n\nThe second proper thing was that I made a friend, which happened much faster than I expected and in a way I did not expect at all. Her name is Yusra and she sits next to me in maths, and we started talking because I couldn't find my ruler and she lent me hers without me even having to ask properly, just sort of noticed and passed it over. That was three weeks ago. Now we walk to the bus stop together most days, and she has already taught me two very useful things: which teachers let you retake a test if you ask nicely, and exactly which bit of the playground wall is warmest to sit against at lunchtime, which matters more than you would think in October.\n\nThe third proper thing, and I promise this is the last one because Mum is telling me the postbox closes soon, is that I still miss home, but slightly less loudly than I did in September. I think that is allowed to be true at the same time as everything above.\n\nLots of love,\nDara","acceptedAnswers":["homesickness has eased slightly over time, but not gone completely","she still misses home but less intensely than at first","feelings have softened from September to now","adjusting gradually, though still missing home a little"],"validationTier":"TIER2_ACCEPTED_SET"}$json$,
 'English Wave 1 Reading Remediation. QT-RC-07 (applied here across the same narrator at two points in time within one letter), competency RC-01. Question family: wave1-fam-comparative-extraction. Drawing on this passage''s own implicit change-over-time, previously untested directly despite the letter''s explicit numbered structure.', 2, 'wave1-eng-lettertonana',
 'wave1-fam-comparative-extraction', 'angel_original', 'provisional', 1, true, 'Describing only the early homesickness OR only the final admission, without identifying the CHANGE (lessening, not disappearance) between them.',
 'FAR_TRANSFER', null),

('w1-letter-09', 'english', 'QT-RC-02', array['csse'], 'medium', 'short-answer', 90,
 $json${"id":"w1-letter-09","marks":2,"skill":"inference","question":"Why might the launderette woman have \"appreciated more than she probably realised\" that Dara thanked her for pointing the way?","modelAnswer":"The woman helped a clearly lost, embarrassed stranger kindly, without laughing at her -- a small act she likely didn't think much of, but which mattered a great deal to Dara on an already difficult first day, so Dara's gratitude was probably more meaningful to the woman than the woman herself would have expected.","passageTitle":"A Letter to Nana","passageText":"Dear Nana,\n\nMum says I have to write to you properly instead of just sending you funny videos, so here is a proper letter, even though I still think the videos were better.\n\nBristol is loud in a different way from home. Not louder exactly, just loud about different things. There is a market near our new flat that starts setting up at six in the morning, and for the first week I woke up every single day thinking someone was dismantling a small building directly under my window. Mum says I will stop noticing it eventually. I do not fully believe her yet, but she was right about the flat being bigger than it looked in the photos, so maybe she will be right about this too.\n\nThe first proper thing that happened was that I got lost on the way to school, on my actual first day, which is possibly the most embarrassing thing that has ever happened to me, and I am including the time I fell off the stage in Year 4. I took a left turn that I was extremely confident about and ended up outside a launderette instead of the school gates. A woman doing her washing pointed me the right way without laughing at me even once, which I appreciated more than she probably realised.\n\nThe second proper thing was that I made a friend, which happened much faster than I expected and in a way I did not expect at all. Her name is Yusra and she sits next to me in maths, and we started talking because I couldn't find my ruler and she lent me hers without me even having to ask properly, just sort of noticed and passed it over. That was three weeks ago. Now we walk to the bus stop together most days, and she has already taught me two very useful things: which teachers let you retake a test if you ask nicely, and exactly which bit of the playground wall is warmest to sit against at lunchtime, which matters more than you would think in October.\n\nThe third proper thing, and I promise this is the last one because Mum is telling me the postbox closes soon, is that I still miss home, but slightly less loudly than I did in September. I think that is allowed to be true at the same time as everything above.\n\nLots of love,\nDara","acceptedAnswers":["a small kindness meant a lot to Dara on a difficult day, more than the woman would have known","the woman probably didn''t realise how much her small act of kindness helped Dara feel less embarrassed","simple kindness to a stranger can matter more to the receiver than the giver realises","Dara was already upset/embarrassed, so the kindness meant more than usual"],"validationTier":"TIER2_ACCEPTED_SET"}$json$,
 'English Wave 1 Reading Remediation. QT-RC-02 (motive/character inference sub-format), competency RC-02. Question family: wave1-fam-motive-inference.', 2, 'wave1-eng-lettertonana',
 'wave1-fam-motive-inference', 'angel_original', 'provisional', 1, true, 'Focusing only on Dara''s own embarrassment without addressing what the phrase asks -- why the woman herself might not have realised the impact of her small kindness.',
